{"stage": "step:s2", "served_model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2638, "output_tokens": 593, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "Two of the three technologies meet the radiation requirement, Sensor-T1 and Sensor-T2. Sensor-T3 does not.\n\n**Radiation environment and requirement**\n- The project documents do not give the fluence or dose at the innermost radius over the HL-LHC lifetime. The only fluence figure is the 2e16 neq/cm\u00b2 used in the irradiation campaign, which I took as the end-of-life reference. I did not find a documented derivation that this matches the innermost-layer radius.\n- The requirement, from `irradiation_testbeam_2026.md`, is a hit efficiency of at least 97% up to end of life.\n\n**Qualification data after 2e16 neq/cm\u00b2 (SPS test beam)**\n- Sensor-T1 (planar, standard pitch): 98.5%. Meets the requirement.\n- Sensor-T2 (3D electrodes, larger pitch): 99.2%. Meets the requirement.\n- Sensor-T3 (thin pitch, large charge sharing): 82.0%. Fails. Charge trapping is strong and full depletion cannot be reached at the maximum bias.\n\n**Unirradiated resolution (2025 summary, 150 \u00b5m)**\n- Sensor-T3: 6.0 \u00b5m, but excluded on radiation grounds.\n- Sensor-T1: 9.0 \u00b5m.\n- Sensor-T2: 14.0 \u00b5m.\n\nSensor-T3 has the best unirradiated resolution but is ruled out. The choice is between T1 and T2. T2 has the better radiation margin, while T1 has the better resolution. The resolution trade-off should be checked with `simulate_resolution` for T1 and T2 across thicknesses. That simulation only models unirradiated sensors, so the radiation data has to be used to decide between them.", "type": "text"}]}
