{"stage": "step:s5", "served_model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2375, "output_tokens": 566, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "**Final recommendation: Sensor-T1 (planar, standard pitch), 150 \u00b5m thick.**\n\n**Resolution (unirradiated, single-hit, from the simulation):**\n- Sensor-T1 at 150 \u00b5m: **8.92 \u00b5m**, the best value across all thicknesses and technologies tested.\n- Nearby T1 thicknesses: 100 \u00b5m gives 9.43 \u00b5m, 200 \u00b5m gives 9.14 \u00b5m, and 300 \u00b5m gives 11.98 \u00b5m.\n- Sensor-T2 is the best alternative at 13.74 \u00b5m (200 \u00b5m), so T1 at 150 \u00b5m is about 35% better.\n- Sensor-T3 was not ranked on resolution because it was excluded on radiation grounds.\n\n**Radiation suitability:**\n- The innermost layer's end-of-life fluence is about 2\u00d710\u00b9\u2076 n_eq/cm\u00b2.\n- The requirement is at least 97% hit efficiency at that fluence.\n- Sensor-T1 reaches 98.5% and Sensor-T2 reaches 99.2%. Both pass.\n- Sensor-T3 reaches only 82%, so it is excluded.\n- Two of the three technologies meet the radiation requirement. Among those two, T1 gives the better resolution.\n\n**Caveat:** The resolution figures are for unirradiated sensors only. Radiation damage reduces charge collection and can degrade resolution, so the in-situ resolution at end of life will be worse than 8.92 \u00b5m. The 98.5% efficiency margin over the 97% requirement is modest. The candidate-spec document does not give radiation-specific details, so the efficiency values come from the earlier simulation step and were not independently checked against the documents.", "type": "text"}]}
